  "original_context": {
    "full_target": "The complete Theorem 1 (Logarithm-free convergence), with its Section 2 standing definitions and fixed scheme, on PDF page 2.\n\nUse the periodic domain mathbbT = reals/(2*pi*integers) with normalized measure dx/(2*pi). For a periodic function f, hat f(k) = (2*pi)^(-1) integral_0^(2*pi) f(x) exp(-i*k*x) dx and <k> = (1+k^2)^(1/2). The Sobolev norm is ||f||_(H^s)^2 = sum_(k in Z) <k>^(2*s) |hat f(k)|^2. These spaces are completions of trigonometric polynomials in these norms, with negative-order spaces interpreted as distributions. Reality means hat f(-k) = conjugate(hat f(k)); mean zero means hat f(0) = 0. Time suprema use continuous representatives; derivative norms use essential suprema.\n\nLet S_t = exp(-t*partial_x^3), with Fourier multiplier exp(i*t*k^3). Let P0 be the spatial mean and P = I-P0. Let D^(-1) = partial_x^(-1) have multiplier (i*k)^(-1) for k != 0 and zero at k=0, and D^(-2) = (D^(-1))^2. The equation is\n  u_t + u_xxx = (1/2)*partial_x(u^2), u(0)=u0.\nThe exact solution is the unique real mean-zero C([0,T];H^gamma) distributional solution obtained by smooth approximation.\n\nFor timestep tau=T/L, the time-semidiscrete scheme is exactly\n  u^0 = u0,\n  u^(n+1) = S_tau u^n + F_tau(u^n) + H_tau(u^n),\nwhere all products below are spatial products and [G(s)]_(s=0)^(s=tau) means G(tau)-G(0):\n  F_tau(f) = (1/6)*P[(S_tau D^(-1) f)^2]\n             - (1/6)*S_tau P[(D^(-1) f)^2],\n  H_tau(f) = (1/3)*P[(S_tau D^(-1) f)*D^(-1) F_tau(f)]\n             + (tau/9)*(S_tau D^(-1) f)*P0(f^2)\n             - (1/54)*[S_(tau-s) D^(-1)[(S_s D^(-1) f)^3]]_(s=0)^(s=tau)\n             - (1/(27*tau))*[S_(tau-s) D^(-2)[(S_(s-tau) D^(-2) F_tau(f))*(S_s D^(-1) f)]]_(s=0)^(s=tau).\nThis is the source scheme (2), with no spatial frequency cutoff.\n\nFor every T>0, 0<gamma<=1 and R>0, there exist C>0 and tau0>0 depending only on T,gamma,R such that, for every real mean-zero u0 in H^gamma(mathbbT) with ||u0||_(H^gamma)<=R and every positive integer L with tau=T/L<=tau0, this fixed scheme satisfies\n  max_(0<=n<=L) ||u(n*tau)-u^n||_(L^2) <= C*tau^gamma.\nThe constants are uniform over the entire stated initial-data ball and all permitted timesteps, with no Fourier-support restriction. The target includes all the hypotheses, quantifiers, endpoints, solution convention and method above.",
